CodeMirror Extended Syntax
==========================

Optional editor highlighting and comment shortcut for the CosmoCode DokuWiki CodeMirror plugin
(2026 module-based version). No DokuWiki core, CodeMirror, or renderer changes.

All definitions live in this plugin's definitions.js, grouped by plugin name.
There are no files to install or maintain in the supported plugins themselves.
Each definition is an object with any of these fields:

  tags: ['figure', 'subfigure', 'caption']
  braces: ['ref', 'autoref']
  mediaExtensions: ['svg']
  tokens: [{match: /^@EXAMPLE@/, style: 'variable-2', sol: false}]
  blocks: [{open: /^\/\*/, close: '*/', style: 'comment', singleLine: false}]
  codeOptions: true

Patterns must match at the current position, consume at least one character,
and have no global/sticky flags. Tags and prefixes are case-sensitive; include
all supported spellings. Blocks protect their contents from wiki highlighting.
Blocks with keyValue: true colour their opening/closing markers, keys, equals
signs, ampersand separators and literal values separately (used by Meta).
Styles are standard CodeMirror styles: tag, attribute, string, keyword, number,
operator, link, meta, comment, etc. The selected editor theme controls colours.

The loader only runs on editor pages and selects rules for installed, enabled
plugins from definitions.js. It does not infer syntax from their PHP code.
It handles asynchronous loading and editor/highlighting toggles through the
upstream codemirror_ready event. Disabling it leaves ordinary CodeMirror intact.
DiffPreview's Changes view reuses CodeMirror's existing editor resource loader,
so the editor and extended highlighting remain available without patching
CodeMirror or DiffPreview. Resources already supplied by CodeMirror are reused.

Supported plugins: approve, bootswrapper, codehighlightjs, commentsyntax, datatables, diagrams,
gallery, icons, iframe, latexcaption, mathjax, meta, pageredirect, qrcodephp, threejs.

Monokai's missing meta colour is supplied by style.css so core page controls
(NOTOC, NOCACHE) and BootsWrapper macros are visible in that theme too.
Comment Syntax's /* ... */ and <!-- ... --> blocks use the theme's muted comment
colour, including across blank lines. Optional nested and // comments are not
supported; //italic// retains its normal DokuWiki highlighting.

With Comment Syntax enabled, Ctrl+/ (Cmd+/ on Mac) toggles a /* ... */ comment
around the current line or selected lines. Press again to uncomment; ordinary
Undo also restores the text. Inside a highlighted code block, CodeMirror uses
that language's comment syntax when available. Read-only editors are unchanged.
Existing editor shortcuts are preserved. The helper is the CodeMirror 5.65.21 comment add-on, bundled under
vendor/codemirror with its MIT license. Its token checks use fresh parsing
instead of cached display styles, so edits across blank lines toggle reliably:
https://github.com/codemirror/codemirror5/blob/5.65.21/addon/comment/comment.js

MathJax highlighting covers $...$, $$...$$, \(...\), \[...\], and the named
environments protected by the MathJax plugin: math, displaymath, equation,
eqnarray, align, flalign, alignat, multline and gather (including supported
starred forms). A named block ends at its matching \end{environment}, including
across blank lines; nested environments with other names do not end it early.
The whole expression uses string and string-2 token styles, so themes such as
Monokai that do not colour string-2 still highlight it as a string.

Highlighting does not change saved text; the comment shortcut edits the source
only when invoked. This is not syntax validation or autocomplete.
Update definitions.js here when adding custom syntax.
Code/file/nowiki and embedded language contents retain their native highlighting.
Supported plugins do not require this integration to render pages.

The tests/mode.test.mjs regression test uses the installed CodeMirror mode:
  node lib/plugins/codemirrorsyntax/tests/mode.test.mjs

The tests/comments.html browser fixture checks actual Ctrl+/ and Cmd+/ events,
selections, blank lines, undo, embedded languages, read-only mode and hook reuse.
With the upstream CodeMirror plugin installed alongside this plugin, run:
  google-chrome --headless --allow-file-access-from-files --dump-dom --virtual-time-budget=10000 file:///absolute/path/to/codemirrorsyntax/tests/comments.html
The result element should report PASS 14 browser checks.

